1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:04,003
A wallet asks you to approve something.

2
00:00:04,003 --> 00:00:06,245
You recognize the project name.

3
00:00:06,245 --> 00:00:08,137
The button looks familiar.

4
00:00:08,137 --> 00:00:10,880
But what exactly are you agreeing to?

5
00:00:11,730 --> 00:00:15,727
Before you use an app on Tari, ask three questions.

6
00:00:15,727 --> 00:00:18,033
What action am I authorizing?

7
00:00:18,033 --> 00:00:20,954
Who has power inside the application?

8
00:00:20,954 --> 00:00:23,410
And can its code change later?

9
00:00:24,260 --> 00:00:25,721
First, the action.

10
00:00:25,721 --> 00:00:28,796
Read the request you actually received.

11
00:00:28,796 --> 00:00:30,641
Which network is it on?

12
00:00:30,641 --> 00:00:33,024
Is this a test, or real funds?

13
00:00:33,024 --> 00:00:37,940
Check the destination, the operation, the amount, and the fee.

14
00:00:38,590 --> 00:00:41,934
Here's a real send screen in Tari Universe.

15
00:00:41,934 --> 00:00:46,721
The address check passes, but the app flags the amount in red,

16
00:00:46,721 --> 00:00:49,761
because this wallet holds no XTM yet.

17
00:00:49,761 --> 00:00:51,129
That's the habit:

18
00:00:51,129 --> 00:00:52,496
read every field,

19
00:00:52,496 --> 00:00:56,750
and if anything doesn't match what you intended, stop.

20
00:00:57,500 --> 00:01:01,448
If the interface doesn't explain something important, pause.

21
00:01:01,448 --> 00:01:03,972
A familiar logo is not an explanation.

22
00:01:03,972 --> 00:01:06,625
And you don't have to diagnose the whole

23
00:01:06,625 --> 00:01:10,140
app before declining a request you don't understand.

24
00:01:11,190 --> 00:01:12,622
Second, authority.

25
00:01:12,622 --> 00:01:13,903
On Tari's Ootle,

26
00:01:13,903 --> 00:01:18,424
a component can set an access rule for each of its methods.

27
00:01:18,424 --> 00:01:23,096
A rule can limit an action to a particular key or credential.

28
00:01:23,096 --> 00:01:24,302
And by default,

29
00:01:24,302 --> 00:01:29,350
any method without a rule is denied to everyone except the owner.

30
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,127
Ownership is set separately,

31
00:01:32,127 --> 00:01:35,208
and the owner can always call any method.

32
00:01:35,208 --> 00:01:39,095
So a public button tells you only part of the story.

33
00:01:39,095 --> 00:01:40,342
Ask the project:

34
00:01:40,342 --> 00:01:44,669
what can an administrator do, that an ordinary user can't?

35
00:01:44,669 --> 00:01:47,680
Look for a clear answer, with evidence.

36
00:01:48,730 --> 00:01:49,965
Third, upgrades.

37
00:01:49,965 --> 00:01:54,398
When an Ootle component is created, it gets an upgrade rule.

38
00:01:54,398 --> 00:01:57,890
And that rule can never be changed afterwards.

39
00:01:58,540 --> 00:02:02,918
Locked, the default, means nobody can migrate the component.

40
00:02:02,918 --> 00:02:04,641
Its code never changes.

41
00:02:04,641 --> 00:02:09,019
Owner means whoever satisfies the owner rule can upgrade it.

42
00:02:09,019 --> 00:02:13,253
And an access rule can hand upgrade power to someone else,

43
00:02:13,253 --> 00:02:15,700
like a multisig, or a DAO badge.

44
00:02:16,350 --> 00:02:20,095
The upgrade rule is part of the component's header.

45
00:02:20,095 --> 00:02:24,920
So wallets and explorers can show you whether the code can change,

46
00:02:24,920 --> 00:02:26,510
and who controls it.

47
00:02:27,260 --> 00:02:30,666
But locked code is not a safety certificate.

48
00:02:30,666 --> 00:02:33,467
It fixes the code, not the behavior.

49
00:02:33,467 --> 00:02:34,754
Bugs can remain,

50
00:02:34,754 --> 00:02:39,900
and settings controlled by an owner can still change what it does.

51
00:02:40,750 --> 00:02:42,999
These are three separate checks.

52
00:02:42,999 --> 00:02:45,656
A request can match what you intended,

53
00:02:45,656 --> 00:02:48,995
while the app still has powerful administrators.

54
00:02:48,995 --> 00:02:52,879
An app can have locked code, and still contain mistakes.

55
00:02:52,879 --> 00:02:57,390
A polished interface answers none of these questions on its own.

56
00:02:58,241 --> 00:02:59,765
So before you approve:

57
00:02:59,765 --> 00:03:03,145
what action, whose authority, and what can change?

58
00:03:03,145 --> 00:03:06,392
If the wallet or the documentation can't answer,

59
00:03:06,392 --> 00:03:09,921
treat that as missing information, not reassurance.

60
00:03:10,771 --> 00:03:12,882
This is an independent explainer

61
00:03:12,882 --> 00:03:15,505
based on Tari's published documentation.

62
00:03:15,505 --> 00:03:18,448
It's not a security audit of any application.

63
00:03:18,448 --> 00:03:20,111
Sources are linked below.

64
00:03:20,111 --> 00:03:21,582
Learn with test funds,

65
00:03:21,582 --> 00:03:24,931
and make every approval a decision you understand.
